---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import MockServerGenerator from '../../components/MockServerGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Mock API Server Config Generator (db.json)" description="Generate a db.json file for json-server-style tools to spin up a fake REST API instantly.">
  <MockServerGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why mock servers speed up frontend development</h2>
  <p>Frontend work often gets blocked waiting for a real backend to be ready — a mock server backed by a simple JSON file lets frontend development proceed against a realistic-looking API immediately, with full CRUD behavior, before the real backend exists.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Define your resources and sample data as JSON arrays.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated db.json.</li>
    <li>Run it with a json-server-style tool to get instant REST endpoints.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A db.json with a "users" array automatically gets full REST endpoints — GET/POST /users, GET/PUT/DELETE /users/:id — without writing any server code.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting mock data doesn't persist meaningfully</strong> across server restarts in some setups — don't rely on it for anything beyond local development testing.</li>
    <li><strong>Using unrealistic sample data</strong> that doesn't match your real API's eventual shape, causing frontend code built against the mock to need rework later.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Developing a frontend before the real backend API is ready</li>
    <li>Creating a quick prototype or demo without building a real backend</li>
    <li>Writing frontend tests against a predictable, controllable fake API</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this suitable for production use?</strong> No — this is strictly a development/prototyping tool; it lacks real authentication, validation, and persistence guarantees needed for production.</p>
</ToolLayout>
